Q1. What is the difference between ArrayList, LinkedList, HashSet, and HashMap? When would you choose each one?

⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️ ArrayList ⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️

ArrayList is backed by a dynamically resizable array. It provides fast random access using an index, typically O(1),
but inserting or removing elements in the middle is O(n) because elements need to be shifted.

I would choose ArrayList when I need an ordered collection, allow duplicates,
and frequently access elements by index. It is generally my default choice for a List.

⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️ Linked list ⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️


LinkedList is based on a doubly linked list. Accessing an element by index is O(n),
but insertion or removal at the beginning or end is O(1) when the node position is already known.

I would use it when I specifically need linked-list or deque-style operations. 
In modern Java applications, however, I would often prefer ArrayDeque for queue or deque use cases rather than choosing LinkedList automatically.


⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️ Hashset ⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️

HashSet stores unique elements and does not guarantee iteration order. It is hash-table based and provides average O(1) lookup, insertion, and deletion.

I would choose HashSet when uniqueness is the primary requirement—for example, maintaining a set of unique user IDs or checking whether an element has already been processed.



⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️ Hashmap ⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️


HashMap stores data as key-value pairs. Keys are unique, while values can be duplicated. It provides average O(1) lookup, insertion, and deletion by key.

I would choose HashMap when I need to associate one piece of information with another—for example, mapping a userId to a User object or maintaining a frequency count.

