Q3. How does HashMap work internally? Explain hashing, buckets, collisions, equals(), and hashCode().



HashMap stores key-value pairs using a hash table. When we insert a key,
HashMap calculates the key's hash using hashCode(),
processes that hash to determine a bucket index, and stores the entry in that bucket. During lookup, 
it calculates the hash again to locate the bucket and then uses equals() to identify the exact key, 
especially when multiple keys are in the same bucket due to collisions. If two different keys map to the same bucket, 
that's called a collision, and HashMap stores both entries in that bucket. In modern Java, 
heavily-collided buckets can be converted from a linked structure into a balanced tree. 
Assuming good hash distribution, get, put, and remove are O(1) on average.




