Q5. Suppose you create an Employee class and override equals() but forget to override hashCode(). What problems can occur when using Employee
as a key in a HashMap or as an element in a HashSet?



It violates the equals-hashCode contract. If two Employee objects are considered equal by equals(), 
they must return the same hashCode(). If hashCode() isn't overridden, 
two logically equal Employee objects may have different hash codes and therefore be placed in different buckets.
In a HashSet, this can allow logically duplicate objects to be stored. In a HashMap,
a logically equal key may fail to retrieve an existing value because the lookup goes to a 
different bucket and never reaches the equals() comparison. Therefore, whenever we override equals(), we should also override hashCode() consistently.

