Q1. What are the four pillars of OOP in Java? Explain each with a practical example.


The four pillars of Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) in Java are:

Encapsulation
Inheritance
Polymorphism
Abstraction


1. Encapsulation


Encapsulation is the practice of bundling data and the methods that operate on that data within a class and 
restricting direct access to the internal state. In Java, this is commonly achieved using private fields with controlled access through methods.

class BankAccount {

    private double balance;

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0) {
            balance += amount;
        }
    }

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}

BankAccount account = new BankAccount();

account.deposit(5000);

System.out.println(account.getBalance());

2. Inheritance

Inheritance allows a class to acquire fields and behavior from another class,
enabling code reuse and specialization. In Java, it is implemented using extends.
For example, Developer can extend Employee and inherit common employee behavior while adding developer-specific functionality."


class Employee {

    String name;
    int employeeId;

    void work() {
        System.out.println("Employee is working");
    }
}

class Developer extends Employee {

    void writeCode() {
        System.out.println("Writing Java code");
    }
}

class Manager extends Employee {

    void conductMeeting() {
        System.out.println("Conducting meeting");
    }
}


Developer developer = new Developer();

developer.name = "Akshay";
developer.employeeId = 101;

developer.work();
developer.writeCode();


Developer gets name, employeeId, and work() from Employee.


3. Polymorphism


Polymorphism means one interface or reference can represent different implementations 
and the same method call can behave differently depending on the actual object. In Java, runtime polymorphism is achieved through method overriding, 
while compile-time polymorphism is achieved through method overloading.

class Employee {

    void work() {
        System.out.println("Employee is working");
    }
}

class Developer extends Employee {

    @Override
    void work() {
        System.out.println("Developer is writing code");
    }
}


class Manager extends Employee {

    @Override
    void work() {
        System.out.println("Manager is managing the team");
    }
}

Employee employee1 = new Developer();
Employee employee2 = new Manager();

employee1.work();
employee2.work();



4. Abstraction : 

Abstraction means exposing essential behavior while hiding implementation details. 
In Java, we commonly achieve it using interfaces and abstract classes.
For example, a PaymentService interface can expose a pay() method while different implementations handle UPI or credit-card processing internally.




