Q 4. Multiple threads need to read and modify a shared HashMap. Would you use HashMap,
Collections.synchronizedMap(), or ConcurrentHashMap? Explain your choice.



If multiple threads need to both read and modify a shared HashMap, I would choose ConcurrentHashMap.

A regular HashMap is not thread-safe. Concurrent reads and writes can lead to race conditions and inconsistent behavior.

Collections.synchronizedMap() makes the map's individual operations synchronized:

Map<String, Integer> map =
    Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>());

This is thread-safe, but it uses synchronization around map operations, which can reduce concurrency because operations are more heavily serialized.

ConcurrentHashMap is specifically designed for concurrent access:

Map<String, Integer> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

map.put("A", 100);
map.put("B", 200);

It allows multiple threads to operate on the map concurrently while maintaining thread safety and generally provides better scalability than a synchronized map.

One important point is that thread-safe individual operations don't automatically make a sequence of operations atomic. For example:




